> Me and my roomate have networked our computers togethor with a router.
> I'm running linux and he has a mac. I've only been running linux for a
> couple of weeks, so I'm still pretty mucha newbie with it. Anyway,
> we've both got Nethack installed but we want to share our bone files
> and high scores with each other. Does anyone know how to set up a
> system like that?

Seems to me the easiest way is to create an account for your roommate
on your linux box.  He can telnet/ssh[1] into your machine and run
your copy of nethack.  Then, all bones file will be written to
$NETHACKDIR/bones on the linux box; all game records go into the same
logfile.

Otherwise, you're kinda up a creek.  Macs are big endian while PCs are
little endian.  Thus, unless you write a script to reverse the bits in
every byte, you cannot share your bones (which are binary) between
machines.  IIRC, you get a version mismatch error when the program on
one platform tries to read bones files created on the other platform.

Good luck
E

[1] I'm assuming he's using OSX and knows how to use terminal.app...
also that you have telnetd, or preferably sshd, running on the linux
machine.

